  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/4/4b/Journey_Into_Mystery_Vol_1_94.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "A rocket carrying a nuclear warhead suddenly goes off course, but they can't detonate it in space, so Thor is summoned.&nbsp; Naturally, it's Loki's doing from Asgard.&nbsp; He wants Thor to destroy the missile, but he distracts Thor as the hammer returns so that it strikes him in the head instead of him catching it - right in the \"chromosomatic gland, which determines and changes personality!\"\n\nThor suddenly flies to Asgard and attacks Heimdall, calling him a \"flunky.\"&nbsp; Thor then sees Loki bound with uru bonds, and forgetting he's Thor's arch-enemy, frees him.&nbsp; Odin and his men confront them, and Loki realizes that the two of them can't defeat all of Asgard, united against them, so he plans to take Thor to Earth and cause havoc, making Earth his \"hostage till you yield control of Asgard to Thor and me!\"\n\nOnce on Earth, Loki has Thor summoning thunder, lightning and so forth \"to destroy man's proudest achievements.\"&nbsp; Soon the Taj Mahal, Eiffel Tower, Golden Gate Bridge, Panama Canal and the Leaning Tower of Pisa are destroyed by Thor while Loki uses his magic to animate the Sphinx, rock the Empire State Building and so forth - it's an absolute rampage on humanity!\n\nFinally, various representatives of the United Nations approach the brothers and beg them to contact Odin to ask that they be allowed to take over Asgard so they'll leave Earth alone.&nbsp; But when Thor contacts Asgard, the hammer suddenly suspends in air and then drops on Thor's head, in the same spot as before.&nbsp; <i>Holy cow</i>!&nbsp; It's actually Odin and the gods of Asgard, <i>not</i> the various members of the U.N after all!&nbsp; (<i>Shock!</i>)\n\nThor is back to normal and swears to the people of Earth that he will repair all the damage.&nbsp; And Odin then adds that the \"memory of these events shall be erased from the minds of men!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "Before I go any farther, can the handle of Thor's hammer <i>possibly</i> get any longer than it is shown on the splash page?&nbsp; I mean, <i>wow</i> - that has to be 3 feet long and easily half of Thor's height!&nbsp; (Contrast that to the later drawings of John Byrne, who I thought always drew the handle way too short!)&nbsp; And I'm loving Loki's evil grin on that splash page as well!\n\nBut as for the story itself, there's just no escaping the fact that this issue is <i>dreadful</i>...just absolutely poor - a completely made-up gland, leading to a nonsensical story premise, all reversed and reset in the space of about 14 pages.&nbsp; <i>Gag!</i>  Destruction of all these landmarks on Earth, all repaired like nothing ever happened to them?&nbsp; <i>Ugh</i>, please stop insulting our intelligence!\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nIt really does seem like Stan had books that he favored in these early days of Marvel and focused heavily on them, but threw out inane plots for some of the other titles and let his scripters run with them.&nbsp; One wonders if he himself cringed when he read some of this dreck!\n\nAnd the handle of that hammer!&nbsp; <i>Holy cow!</i>",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}